picolibc's <sys/ioctl.h> has no FIONBIO, the ioctl that turns a
descriptor's non-blocking mode on or off. ioctl() already passes an
unknown request straight to the kernel (patch 30), and the kernel has
FIONBIO at Linux's number, so all that was missing is the name.

It mattered to OpenSSL: BIO_socket_nbio() uses FIONBIO when it is
defined and fcntl(O_NONBLOCK) otherwise -- but bio_sock.c does not
include <fcntl.h>, so on this system neither branch compiled, and the
function became an unconditional "passed invalid argument". Every
`openssl s_client -connect` failed before sending a byte. curl was not
affected: it sets non-blocking mode itself, with fcntl.

--- a/libc/include/sys/ioctl.h	2026-09-26 17:00:34.027003868 -0600
+++ b/libc/include/sys/ioctl.h	2026-09-26 17:00:34.053580024 -0600
@@ -71,6 +71,15 @@
 #define TIOCSPTLCK  0x40045431 /* unlockpt: int *                    */
 
 /*
+ * Non-blocking mode for a descriptor, from an int: 1 on, 0 off. The
+ * same as fcntl(O_NONBLOCK), and what socket code reaches for first --
+ * OpenSSL's BIO_socket_nbio() tests for FIONBIO and, finding neither
+ * it nor <fcntl.h> in scope, compiled to "passed invalid argument", so
+ * `openssl s_client` could not connect to anything.
+ */
+#define FIONBIO     0x5421
+
+/*
  * WHAT TERMINAL IS THIS DESCRIPTOR OPEN ON -- the name as /dev has it,
  * without the "/dev/": "console", "ttyS0", "pts/0". Fills a
  * char[TTYNAME_MAX]. This one is NOT Linux's, because Linux has no
